\section{Playback and tagging}
\label{tut:playback-and-tagging}

This tutorial focuses on two related reading tools: playback for
reviewing the log and tags for naming important commits.
The learner already has a recorded project, so the new contrast is not
between \enquote{before and after learnlog}, but between browsing the
entire history and jumping directly to a named checkpoint.
The tutorial varies only the starting point of playback---first the
whole log, then playback from a tag---while the recorded project stays
invariant, so the learner discerns that tags are not extra metadata on
the side; they are navigation anchors into the existing commit history.
The steps separate the two tools before fusing them: batch playback
alone, then tagging alone, then playback that starts from the tag,
first in batch mode and finally in the interactive viewer.
The last two steps form their own small generalisation: the medium
varies (batch print against interactive viewer) while the tag-anchored
starting point stays invariant, so the learner sees that the anchor
belongs to the log, not to one viewing tool.

\subsection{Two variants, one contract}

As in \cref{tut:getting-started}, the tutorial tangles into a novice
entry, [[playback-and-tagging]], and an expert entry,
[[playback-and-tagging-expert]], that share every step's checks
through common chunks.
Here the mechanism the expert variant makes visible is the log
itself: that reviewing is recorded, that a tag is a lightweight Git
tag in the hidden repository, and that playback resolves a tag to the
run it names.
The novice variant keeps to the reading workflow and never mentions
Git.

